# Analysis

The original trigger has two problems:
1. **Before loop**: One SOQL per contact → 200 queries on a 200-contact load.
2. **After loop**: One SOQL **and** one DML per contact → 200 queries + up to 200 updates.

The fix: batch the `AccountId` set, issue **one SOQL per context** (2 total), and collect accounts into a list for a **single `update`** (1 total). The "first contact in trigger order wins" semantics for the phone-setting logic is preserved by iterating `Trigger.new` sequentially and using a `claimed` set so no account is updated twice.

## File: force-app/main/default/triggers/ContactTrigger.trigger

```apex
trigger ContactTrigger on Contact (before insert, before update, after insert) {
    if (Trigger.isBefore && Trigger.isInsert) {
        ContactTriggerHandler.handleBeforeInsert(Trigger.new);
    }
    if (Trigger.isBefore && Trigger.isUpdate) {
        ContactTriggerHandler.handleBeforeUpdate(Trigger.new, Trigger.oldMap);
    }
    if (Trigger.isAfter && Trigger.isInsert) {
        ContactTriggerHandler.handleAfterInsert(Trigger.new);
    }
}
```

## File: force-app/main/default/classes/ContactTriggerHandler.cls

```apex
/**
 * Bulk-safe handler for ContactTrigger.
 *
 * SOQL budget : 1 query in before, 1 query in after  = 2 total
 * DML budget  : 1 update in after                     = 1 total
 *
 * Behaviour preserved from the original trigger:
 *   Before – copy Account.BillingCountry → Contact.MailingCountry when blank;
 *            copy Account.AccountSource → Contact.LeadSource when blank (insert only).
 *   After  – first contact (in trigger order) with a Phone whose Account has a
 *            blank Phone sets that Account's Phone (single bulk update).
 */
public with sharing class ContactTriggerHandler {

    /* ------------------------------------------------------------------ */
    /*  BEFORE – insert / update                                          */
    /* ------------------------------------------------------------------ */

    public static void handleBeforeInsert(List<Contact> newContacts) {
        applyBeforeLogic(newContacts, true);
    }

    public static void handleBeforeUpdate(List<Contact> newContacts, Map<Id, Contact> oldMap) {
        applyBeforeLogic(newContacts, false);
    }

    private static void applyBeforeLogic(List<Contact> contacts, Boolean isInsert) {
        Set<Id> accountIds = new Set<Id>();
        for (Contact c : contacts) {
            if (c.AccountId != null) {
                accountIds.add(c.AccountId);
            }
        }

        if (accountIds.isEmpty()) {
            return;
        }

        // 1 SOQL
        Map<Id, Account> accountMap = new Map<Id, Account>(
            [SELECT Id, BillingCountry, AccountSource
             FROM Account
             WHERE Id IN :accountIds]
        );

        for (Contact c : contacts) {
            if (c.AccountId == null) {
                continue;
            }
            Account a = accountMap.get(c.AccountId);
            if (a == null) {
                continue;
            }

            if (String.isBlank(c.MailingCountry)) {
                c.MailingCountry = a.BillingCountry;
            }
            if (isInsert && String.isBlank(c.LeadSource)) {
                c.LeadSource = a.AccountSource;
            }
        }
    }

    /* ------------------------------------------------------------------ */
    /*  AFTER – insert                                                    */
    /* ------------------------------------------------------------------ */

    public static void handleAfterInsert(List<Contact> newContacts) {
        // Gather only the accounts we might need to touch
        Set<Id> candidateAccountIds = new Set<Id>();
        for (Contact c : newContacts) {
            if (c.AccountId != null && String.isNotBlank(c.Phone)) {
                candidateAccountIds.add(c.AccountId);
            }
        }

        if (candidateAccountIds.isEmpty()) {
            return;
        }

        // 1 SOQL
        Map<Id, Account> accountMap = new Map<Id, Account>(
            [SELECT Id, Phone
             FROM Account
             WHERE Id IN :candidateAccountIds]
        );

        // Process in trigger order; first contact per account wins
        Set<Id> claimedAccountIds = new Set<Id>();
        List<Account> accountsToUpdate = new List<Account>();

        for (Contact c : newContacts) {
            if (c.AccountId == null
                    || String.isBlank(c.Phone)
                    || claimedAccountIds.contains(c.AccountId)) {
                continue;
            }

            Account a = accountMap.get(c.AccountId);
            if (a != null && String.isBlank(a.Phone)) {
                a.Phone = c.Phone;
                claimedAccountIds.add(c.AccountId);
                accountsToUpdate.add(a);
            }
        }

        // 1 DML
        if (!accountsToUpdate.isEmpty()) {
            update accountsToUpdate;
        }
    }
}
```

### How the "first-in-order" rule is preserved

| Original (row-by-row) | Refactored (batch) |
|---|---|
| Loop iterates `Trigger.new` in natural order | Same `for (Contact c : newContacts)` loop |
| Each iteration re-queries the Account, so a prior `update` is visible | A `claimedAccountIds` set plays the same role: once an account's phone is set, the id is added and every subsequent contact for that account is skipped |
| Only the first qualifying contact ever calls `update a` | Only the first qualifying contact adds the account to `accountsToUpdate` |

### Budget check (200 contacts)

* **Before:** 1 SOQL (`SELECT … FROM Account WHERE Id IN :accountIds`)
* **After:** 1 SOQL (`SELECT … FROM Account WHERE Id IN :candidateAccountIds`)
* **DML:** 1 `update accountsToUpdate`
* **Total: 2 SOQL · 1 DML** ✓

