/**
 * Service class for importing contacts from a legacy system.
 * Inserts contacts in a single bulk-safe DML while skipping the welcome-task
 * action via TAF's bypass API, then restores the action so subsequent inserts
 * in the same transaction are unaffected.
 */
public class ContactImportService {

    private static final String ACTION_TO_SKIP = 'TA_Contact_CreateWelcomeTask';

    public static void importContacts(List<Contact> contacts) {
        if (contacts == null || contacts.isEmpty()) {
            return;
        }

        // Deactivate only the welcome-task action.  The before-insert
        // formatting action (TA_Contact_FormatPhone) and every other piece
        // of Contact automation continue to execute normally.
        TriggerActionsContext.triggerActionsContext().deactivateAction(ACTION_TO_SKIP);

        try {
            insert contacts;   // single DML – bulk-safe
        } finally {
            // Reactivate so that any contacts inserted later in this
            // transaction (or in a calling context) still get their welcome task.
            TriggerActionsContext.triggerActionsContext().activateAction(ACTION_TO_SKIP);
        }
    }
}
```

**How this satisfies each requirement:**

| Requirement | How it's met |
|---|---|
| Single DML, bulk-safe | One `insert contacts;` on the whole list—no per-record loop. |
| Skip only `TA_Contact_CreateWelcomeTask` | TAF's `deactivateAction` targets that specific registered action by name; all other actions (including the before-insert phone formatter) remain active. |
| Phone formatting still runs | Because `TA_Contact_FormatPhone` was never deactivated, the before-insert trigger fires it as normal. |
| Bypass is temporary / scoped to this call | The `activateAction` in the `finally` block restores the action before the method returns, regardless of whether the insert succeeded or threw. |
| Later inserts in the same transaction unaffected | Once `activateAction` runs, the TAF context no longer lists the action as deactivated, so the next `insert` (or upsert) on Contact will create welcome tasks as usual. |

